    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d521">

                <pb facs="0630" n="558" sameAs="frus:frus1909/pg558"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 18150.</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to
                        the Turkish Ambassador.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Department of State</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1909-02-20"><hi rendition="#italic">February 20,
                        1909</hi></date>.</dateline></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Excellency</hi>: I have the honor to transmit
                    herewith a copy of a letter<anchor n="1" xml:id="d521-f1"/> from Mr. Joseph
                    Boyajian, a naturalized American citizen of Ottoman nativity, stating that the
                    Turkish consul general at New York declines to sign his passport. A copy of Mr.
                    Boyajian’s application for a passport is also inclosed.</p>
                <p>The department would be glad to learn that Mr. Boyajian’s passport could be
                    viséd, and will esteem it a favor if you will inform it whether anything can be
                    done toward bringing this about.</p>
                <p>Accept, etc.,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Robert Bacon.</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
